\title{Implementing Directory-based MESI in Murphi}
\tag{public}
\tag{blog}
\meta{reading-time}{true}

\p{[[CMAL]] is an explicit-state model checker: you describe a system as guarded transition rules, state the invariants, and it enumerates every reachable interleaving looking for a violation. That makes it a natural fit for [cache coherence](GA7N), where the steady-state behavior is easy and the races are not. This post builds a [directory-based](KIT8) [[30XT]] in Murphi, starting from MSI and adding state every time the checker finds a way to break it.}

\p{The state tables come from [A Primer on Memory Consistency and Cache Coherence](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~markhill/papers/primer2020_2nd_edition.pdf), which doubles as the source for these notes. The recurring lesson is that the published tables are not quite enough: every protocol below needs [transient states](Z32G) the tables leave implicit.}

\put\transclude/metadata{false}

\transclude{CMAL}

\section{Why three virtual channels}{
\p{The valid-invalid protocol shipped with the Murphi docs uses two [[HUDE]]. A directory protocol needs three: one each for requests, forwarded requests, and responses. The reason is that [deadlock is a cycle in the dependency graph](DWV1), so it suffices to exhibit one.}
\ul{
  \li{A node issues \code{GetS} to the directory, giving an edge #{N_1 \to D}.}
  \li{The directory forwards \code{Fwd-GetS} to the owner, extending the chain to #{N_1 \to D \to N_2}.}
  \li{The owner sends data back to the requester, closing the cycle #{N_1 \to D \to N_2 \to N_1}.}
}
\p{The data closing that cycle would travel on the same channel as the requests waiting behind it, so a full request buffer wedges the response that would drain it. Partitioning by message class breaks the cycle:}
\ul{
  \li{\strong{Request}: \code{GetS}, \code{GetM}, \code{PutS}, \code{PutM}}
  \li{\strong{Forwarded}: \code{Fwd-GetS}, \code{Fwd-GetM}, \code{Inv}, \code{PutAck}}
  \li{\strong{Response}: \code{Data}, \code{InvAck}}
}
\p{These are enumerated directly in the model's \code{MessageType}.}
\codeblockf{msi.m}{lua}{MessageType: enum {
  GetS,           -- request for shared copy
  GetM,           -- request for modified (exclusive) copy
  PutS,           -- evict shared copy
  PutM,           -- evict modified copy (w/ data)

  FwdGetS,        -- forwarded GetS to owner
  FwdGetM,        -- forwarded GetM to owner
  Inv,            -- invalidation request

  Data,           -- data response
  InvAck,         -- invalidation acknowledgment
  PutAck          -- put acknowledgment
};}
}

